course: dictionary
lesson: 1564
title: A French word in English
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words in English come from other languages. «Chapeau» is a French word for hat. English speakers borrowed it and sometimes use it in their own way, so it is not really an English word.
explain: In French, «un chapeau» means a hat, and «une casquette» means a cap. The ending gives a clue to the little word in front. Words ending in -eau are almost always masculine, so they take «un». Words ending in -ette are almost always feminine, so they take «une».
explain: English speakers also say «Chapeau!» on its own to praise someone, like "Hats off to you! Well done!" Cycling fans use it after a great ride. Say the French word as "sha-POH" when you use it.
fact: «Chapeau» is the French word for hat, said "sha-POH".
fact: «Un chapeau» means a hat, and «une casquette» means a cap.
fact: Words ending in -eau are almost always masculine, like «un manteau» (a coat).
fact: Words ending in -ette are almost always feminine, like «une casquette».
fact: «Chapeau!» is informal English praise that means "Well done!"
exercise: In French, «un chapeau» means what? | *A hat | A scarf | A cap | A coat
exercise: The word «chapeau» ends in -eau. What does that ending suggest? | *Usually masculine, so it takes «un» | It is always a verb | It means something to eat | Usually feminine, so it takes «une»
exercise: A cyclist finishes a hard race and a fan shouts «Chapeau!». What is the fan doing? | Calling for a taxi | Warning the rider about the weather | *Praising the rider and saying well done | Asking for a hat
test: In French, what does «une casquette» mean, and what gender is it likely to be? | *A cap, feminine | A cap, masculine | A hat, masculine | A coat, masculine
test: Which phrase might an English speaker use to praise a great effort? | Un manteau! | Bonjour! | Merci beaucoup! | *Chapeau!
exam: Why does «un» go before «chapeau» but «une» go before «casquette»? | *Because -eau words are almost always masculine and -ette words are almost always feminine | Because «casquette» is a hat | Because «chapeau» is a longer word | Because «un» means hat and «une» means cap
